   ],
   "gap": "def:p15-regular-conditional-law (handbook/parts/p15/regular-conditional-laws.tex lines 49-51) asserts that the regular-conditional-probability special case (S=Omega, target sigma-algebra F_0, X the identity) 'needs an existence hypothesis on the outcome space itself', i.e. that a regular conditional probability need not exist without a hypothesis on (Omega,F_0). No file on the handbook route demonstrates this: no probability space and sub-sigma-algebra G are given for which no probability kernel from (Omega,G) to (Omega,F_0) satisfies the defining identities, and no argument for that necessity is written. The only support is a citation (w02b-p15-008 body lines 235-239, Durrett p. 214, which records that r.c.d.'s do not always exist and credits Dieudonne 1948 without giving the example): a citation where a demonstration is owed. The countable-cocountable model (w02b-p15-008 annex lines 171-269) does not fill it, because a regular conditional law (K_0) exists in that model; item 4 of thm:p15-w02b-p15-022-presentable (exchangeability-and-predictive-induction-w02b-p15-022.tex lines 1048-1064) is conditional on a non-presentable exchangeable law that is not exhibited. Owed: a written model in which no regular conditional probability exists, with its complete proof, or a written argument that establishes the stated need for a hypothesis.",
